{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"# `src/rebar/_commands/fsck.py` is at exactly 800/800 LOC\n\n## Context / Problem\n\n`src/rebar/_commands/fsck.py` is at **exactly 800** lines — the hard cap in\n`.github/module-size-limit.txt`, enforced by the CI module-size gate with no allowlist. Any\nchange to fsck of any size, including a one-line bug fix, cannot land today. This is the third\ninstance of the same pattern (`valiant-balladic-willet` did `passes.py`,\n`splurgy-witless-flamingo` did `_ref_lock.py`), so the remediation is framed as an\narchitectural rework of the fsck cluster rather than a mechanical split: the file is pinned\nbecause four concerns are fused in it, and each new check grows the fused whole.\n\nThe full concern map, the recurrence mechanism and the rationale for each cut are recorded as\nthe architectural-analysis comment on this ticket. This description states the resulting plan.\n\n## Approach\n\nSeparate `fsck.py` by concern along call-graph seams that already exist. `fsck.py` today holds\nfour concerns; two of them already have a single internal call site, which is the seam a prior\nauthor cut and stopped at.\n\n1. **Enumeration + per-ticket validators** move to a new `src/rebar/_commands/fsck_scan.py`:\n   `_scan` (the store walk — JSON validity, CREATE presence, stale index.lock, snapshot\n   consistency, forward-compat event types, and the advisory ensures/authorship tallies) and\n   `_check_snapshot`, which `_scan` is the only caller of. This is the cluster that grows with\n   every check added.\n2. **Tracker-level health** moves to a new `src/rebar/_commands/fsck_tracker_health.py`:\n   `_tracker_health` and the four checks it fans out to — `_tracker_sync_status`,\n   `_branch_mismatch`, `_foreign_store_paths`, `foreign_store_path_list` — plus the\n   `_FSCK_GIT_TIMEOUT` watchdog they share. `_tracker_health` is already the single entry\n   point, called once from `_scan`; these checks share a shape the per-ticket checks do not\n   (each inspects the tracker as a whole via git/config, each yields at most one line, each\n   decides for itself whether that line is counted).\nWithin `fsck_scan.py` the per-check separation is carried through rather than stopping at the\nfile boundary: `_scan` becomes a thin orchestrator over one function per check\n(`_check_json_validity`, `_check_create_events`, `_check_index_lock`, `_check_ticket_snapshots`,\n`_check_forward_compat`, `_advisory_lines`), and `_check_snapshot` sheds its filename-parsing\nloop to `_active_event_map`. This is required as well as cleaner: the complexity ratchet\n(`scripts/check_complexity_baseline.py`) keys accepted debt by `path::symbol`, and its CI `--lock`\nmode refuses **any** new key — so moving the two baselined functions wholesale would read as newly\nadded debt needing an administrator override. Decomposing them under the C901 threshold instead\nmeans `.github/complexity-baseline.json` **loses** both fsck entries and gains none, retiring 43\npoints of recorded debt rather than relocating it.\n\n3. **Report shaping + command driver** stay in `fsck.py`: `_transform_json` /\n   `_STRUCTURED_KINDS` plus `fsck_cli`, `_repair_cli`, `_run_live_repair`,\n   `_missing_tracker_result`. Report shaping is roughly 45 LOC on its own, so extracting it\n   would create a sub-100-LOC module, which the module-size policy forbids; it is also the\n   output half of the same command surface the driver owns. `fsck.py` therefore becomes what\n   its name should mean — the fsck **command**: parse argv, drive scan or repair, render.\n\nDependency direction is strictly one-way and matches the existing call graph: `fsck.py` ->\n`fsck_scan.py` -> {`fsck_tracker_health.py`, `fsck_repair.py`, `fsck_authorship.py`}. No new\nedges, no cycles.\n\nThe change is **behaviour-preserving**: identical findings, identical text and JSON output,\nidentical exit codes. No check logic, message string, or counted/informational classification\nis altered — code moves, and imports are rewired.\n\n`fsck.<symbol>` attribute access is load-bearing outside the module: `_store/gitutil.py` and\n`_commands/fsck_recover.py` import `_resolve_tracker_git_dir`, `_commands/tracker_maintenance.py`\nimports `foreign_store_path_list`, and tests call `fsck._check_snapshot`, `fsck._scan`,\n`fsck._tracker_sync_status`, `fsck._foreign_store_paths`. Every one **calls** the symbol; none\nmonkeypatch it, so `fsck.py` keeps re-exports for all of them and those callers stay untouched.\nThe two symbols that ARE monkeypatched on `fsck` (`_reconciler_in_flight`, `owned_repair_pause`)\nare consumed by `_run_live_repair`, which stays in `fsck.py` — so the trap flamingo recorded, a\nmoved function resolving a patched dependency from the wrong module's globals, does not arise.\n\nOut of scope: `_engine_support/bridge_fsck.py` (a different command, no call edge here) and\n`fsck_repair.py` (already a clean single-concern extraction).\n\n## Scope\n\n- `src/rebar/_commands/fsck.py` — becomes the command driver + report shaping; keeps\n  re-exports for external `fsck.<symbol>` callers.\n- `src/rebar/_commands/fsck_scan.py` — new; `_scan` + `_check_snapshot`.\n- `src/rebar/_commands/fsck_tracker_health.py` — new; `_tracker_health`, `_tracker_sync_status`,\n  `_branch_mismatch`, `_foreign_store_paths`, `foreign_store_path_list`, `_FSCK_GIT_TIMEOUT`.\n- `.github/complexity-baseline.json` — drops the two `fsck.py` entries the decomposition retires.\n\nNo other file changes. Tests are not edited: their behavioural assertions and their\n`fsck.<symbol>` bindings must keep passing unchanged, which is the primary evidence that the\nrefactor preserved behaviour.\n\n## Testing\n\nNo new testable behaviour is introduced — this is a pure structural refactor, so the existing\nsuite is the oracle and is run unmodified:\n\n- The fsck suites (`tests/interfaces/store/test_fsck_*.py`,\n  `tests/interfaces/store/test_repair_pause_ownership_heldout.py`,\n  `tests/interfaces/lifecycle/test_creation_channel_compaction.py`,\n  `tests/interfaces/store/test_fsck_snapshot_checks.py`) pass unchanged, covering both output\n  formats, the repair paths, and the direct `fsck._check_snapshot` / `fsck._scan` bindings.\n- `tests/unit/test_module_size_contract.py` passes (the module-size contract).\n- The full CI selection `pytest -m \"not integration and not external\"` passes, catching any\n  caller whose `fsck.<symbol>` binding the move broke.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] AC1: `src/rebar/_commands/fsck.py` is at most 400 lines — verified by\n      `[ \"$(wc -l < src/rebar/_commands/fsck.py)\" -le 400 ]` exiting 0.\n- [ ] AC2: each new module is at least 100 lines, satisfying the AGENTS.md module-size floor —\n      verified by `[ \"$(wc -l < src/rebar/_commands/fsck_scan.py)\" -ge 100 ]` and the same\n      check on `fsck_tracker_health.py` each exiting 0. (The floor is an AGENTS.md guideline\n      with no test or CI gate behind it, so `wc -l` is the whole check; the module-size\n      contract test asserts only the 800-line upper cap.)\n- [ ] AC3: no `src/rebar` file exceeds the 800-line hard cap — verified by\n      `pytest tests/unit/test_module_size_contract.py` exiting 0.\n- [ ] AC4: `fsck.py` still resolves `_resolve_tracker_git_dir`, `foreign_store_path_list`,\n      `_check_snapshot`, `_scan`, `_tracker_sync_status` and `_foreign_store_paths` as module\n      attributes — verified by\n      `python -c \"from rebar._commands import fsck; [getattr(fsck, n) for n in (...)]\"` exiting 0.\n- [ ] AC5: the external callers of those attributes are untouched — verified by\n      `git diff --stat origin/main -- src/rebar/_store/gitutil.py src/rebar/_commands/fsck_recover.py src/rebar/_commands/tracker_maintenance.py`\n      producing no output.\n- [ ] AC6: no test file is edited — verified by `git diff --stat origin/main -- tests/`\n      producing no output.\n- [ ] AC7: the fsck suites pass unmodified — verified by\n      `pytest tests/interfaces/store/ tests/interfaces/lifecycle/test_creation_channel_compaction.py`\n      exiting 0.\n- [ ] AC8: `make lint` exits 0.\n- [ ] AC9: `make typecheck` exits 0.\n- [ ] AC10: the ten `scripts/check_*.py` gates and the CI pytest selection\n      (`pytest -m \"not integration and not external\"`) each exit 0."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1786605183940028001,"uuid":"8795852b-a88b-4d7e-833f-71c93bb4671e"}